Abstract—The formation of copper-type or grain-scale shear bands in $\mathbf{Al-lMg}$ during plane strain compression (PSC) is described as a function of strain, strain rate and deformation temperature. Shear banding is observed to decrease dramatically in strength over a small range of the Zener–Hollomon parameter $\left(Z\right)$ at $Z$ approximately equal to $1.0{\times}10^{14}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . This sharp transition in shear band formation is seen to correlate exactly with a sharp increase in dynamic recovery during deformation, as inferred from flow stress measurements, hardness measurements and microstructural analysis of as-deformed specimens. The observed correlation of shear band formation and dynamic recovery during deformation provides further insight into the basic mechanisms of shear band formation. The influence of shear bands on deformation texture is also investigated and is seen to affect the relative intensities of, and precise position along, the $\upbeta.$ -fibre. $\circledcirc$ 2001 Acta Materialia Inc. Published by Elsevier Science Ltd. All rights reserved.
Keywords: Shear bands; Texture; Aluminium; Hot deformatio
## 1. INTRODUCTION
Shear bands were first described by Adcock in 1922 [1]. Since then shear bands have been observed to occur in many different metals and alloys, mostly under plane strain deformation conditions that pertain to rolling deformation. The shear bands occur at a variety of angles to the rolling plane, with a typical range between $20^{\circ}$ and $40^{\circ}$ , while an average value of $35^{\circ}$ is generally reported. Although the nature of these shear bands is not entirely clarified yet, it has been observed that deformation is strongly localised and concentrated within a developing shear band. In materials with low stacking fault energy (SFE) like brass or austenitic steels, the onset of mechanical twinning gives rise to the massive formation of socalled brass-type shear bands that extend basically over the entire sample thickness (see, e.g., [2]). In contrast, materials with medium to high SFE may form the so-called copper-type shear bands, which are generally restricted to one or at the most a few contiguous grains [3–22]. Once present in the deformation microstructure, shear bands are thought to affect the recrystallisation behaviour that occurs during subsequent annealing, and thus have a very important effect on properties such as recrystallised grain size and texture. The present paper is concerned with the shear band formation in a magnesium-containing aluminium alloy as function of deformation variables that include strain, strain rate and temperature. Their effect on recrystallisation behaviour is discussed in a subsequent paper [23].
## 2. BACKGROUND
Copper-type shear bands are generally categorised as forming at medium to high strains but have been observed for strains as low as $\varepsilon=0.3$ $25\%$ reduction) [3]. Their strength and frequency of formation, however, are strongly linked to an increase in strain. Furthermore, at low strains they are often observed to be restricted to single grains whereas at higher strains they appear to cross grain boundaries and are observed over several adjacent grains. The tendency for shear banding to occur has not only been linked to composition but also to initial grain size, temperature of deformation and grain orientation. Ridha and Hutchinson [4] observed that the tendency for shear banding to occur increased as initial grain size increased during the cold rolling of copper. Similar observations were made by Korbel et al. [5] for Al– $4.8\mathrm{Mg}$ and by Engler [6] for Al–1.8Cu. Ridha and Hutchinson [4] also observed that the tendency for shear band formation in copper decreased as the deformation temperature increased from $-100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to room temperature, while Nakayama and Morii [7] and Engler et al. [8] reported similar observations for experiments on $\mathbf{Al-}3\mathbf{Mg}$ as the rolling temperature was raised from room temperature to $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The dependency of shear banding occurrence on grain orientation was clearly demonstrated by Morii et al. [9] during experiments on single crystals of $\mathbf{Al-}3\mathbf{Mg}$ . Shear banding was observed for single crystals with a (211)[1¯11] copper orientation, but not for single crystals with a (011)[1¯00] Goss orientation. Similar results have been reported by Engler et al. [8] in Al– 3Mg polycrystals.
There does not appear to be a single accepted theory that fully explains the formation of coppertype shear bands. There are theories based on continuum mechanics [24–29] that attempt to predict the formation of shear bands and in particular the angle to the rolling plane at which they form. For these theories, plasticity is described mathematically from continuum shear flows that occur along the various slip systems of a crystal and discrete dislocation motion is therefore not explicitly modelled. Other theories and observations [5, 7, 9–11, 20–22] attempt to describe shear banding at a microstructural level. Such theories are based on the observation that shear banding appears to be linked to, or preceded by, the formation of microbands in the dislocation structure of the material concerned. The exact nature of the link between microbanding and shear banding is not fully understood. One point of view, after Nakayama and Morii [7], is that groupings of parallel microbands forming a lamellae structure cause longrange internal stresses that lead to the co-operative movement of dislocations, and that this results in shear processes. Korbel and Martin [10] have suggested that groups of microbands generate large stress concentrations at their tips and emit an elasto-plastic impulse on meeting a grain boundary, and are thus able to penetrate a neighbouring grain. Consequently, the crystallographic nature of the microbands is lost and non-crystallographic shear bands form. More recently, Wagner et al. [12, 13] have assumed that microbands act as obstacles to dislocation glide on slip systems that intersect the microbands, and therefore glide is restricted to slip systems parallel to them. This restricted glide then leads to strong shear in the vicinity of the microbands.
If it is accepted that microbands, or more accurately clustering of microbands, are an essential precursor to shear banding, then it is necessary to consider the theories describing microband formation. Microband formation has been attributed to the asymmetry of slip for certain grain orientations, where double the amount of slip has to occur on the two co-planar (CP) systems compared with the two codirectional (CD) systems [9]. The localisation of slip on the CP planes is thought to cause microbands to form parallel to the CP slip systems. It has also been suggested [5] that microband formation is essentially a form of dynamic recovery during deformation. A normal recovered cell structure is prevented from forming by the pinning of dislocations by alloying element atoms and hence microbands form due to the co-operative movement of pinned dislocations.
## 3. EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURE
The material investigated in the present study was a commercial-purity aluminium alloy with a nominal $1~\mathrm{wt}\%~\mathrm{Mg}$ addition. The chemical composition of the alloy is indicated in Table 1 from which it may be observed that besides containing $\mathbf{Mg}$ , the alloy also contains significant amounts of Fe and Si—elements known to form constituent second-phase particles. The material was provided by Alcan International, Banbury, England in cast form and thereafter underwent hot rolling performed by MINTEK, Randburg, South Africa according to a rolling schedule proposed by the authors. Following hot rolling the material was annealed for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ in an air furnace at a temperature of $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Unfortunately—unlike in some earlier test annealing—this treatment was not sufficient to fully recrystallise the specimens. Thus, the starting material consisted of a partially recrystallised microstructure, which has to be taken into consideration when comparing the present results with results from the literature. However, because of the intense recovery taking place in the non-recrystallised regions it is expected that the results obtained throughout this study are not severely affected by the inhomogeneous initial microstructure. Rectangular specimens, $52~\mathrm{mm}{\times}33~\mathrm{mm}{\times}10~\mathrm{mm}$ in size, were machined for plane strain compression (PSC) testing on a purposebuilt apparatus attached to an electro-servohydraulic (ESH) universal testing machine (see Fig. 1) [30]. The design of the apparatus allowed for controlled, uniform compression of specimens at a constant strain rate. The platen/specimen geometry provided for a contact area between platens and specimen of $52~\mathrm{mm}{\times}13~\mathrm{mm}$ (platen breadth) and thus ensured that the strain distribution within the specimens was acceptably uniform, while minimising lateral spread in order to generate a plane strain condition. Heating of specimens was achieved by means of direct resistance heating, and rapid (a time lapse of about 1 s) quenching of these specimens following deformation was possible. Plane strain compression tests were conducted for a matrix of deformation variables: for strains of $\varepsilon=0.25$ to 3.1, for temperatures from room temperature to $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , and at a constant strain rate of $\dot{\varepsilon}=1\mathrm{~s~}^{-1}$ . Tests at strain rates of $0.1\mathrm{~s~}^{-1}$ and $7.5~\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ were also conducted for certain combinations of strain and deformation temperature.
In order to observe the deformation microstructure following deformation, specimens were viewed in the were calculated by the series expansion method $(l_{\mathrm{max}}=22)$ ; the orientations $g$ are given by the Euler angles $\varphi_{1},\Phi,\varphi_{2}$ [31]. Complete ghost-corrected ODFs were derived according to the non-negativity method [32]. In accordance with the orthorhombic sample symmetry in PSC deformation, the textures were represented with iso-intensity lines in equidistant sections $(\Delta\varphi_{2}=5^{\circ})$ ) through the reduced Euler angle space with $0^{\circ}\le\{\varphi_{1},\Phi,\varphi_{2}\}\le90^{\circ}$ .
