4.1.1. The effect of strain. The strength of shear band formation at room temperature and low temperatures $(\leq250^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ of deformation was found to be proportional to the amount of strain experienced during compression, as reflected in Fig. 2. For high strain levels, namely $\varepsilon=2.7$ and $\varepsilon=3.1$ , shear banding was observed in nearly all grains and shear bands were observed to cross several adjacent grains [see Fig.
2(a)]. The strength of the shear banding could be inferred from the very clear contrast of the bands in the optical micrographs (resulting from polarisation differences at the anodic surface of the samples due to orientation differences in the shear bands), and also from the degree of distortion of the grain boundaries by the shear bands themselves. Both these effects are visible in Fig. 2(a). For medium amounts of strain, $\varepsilon=1.6$ , the strength of shear banding was still observed to be strong compared with higher strain levels, although less distortion of grain boundaries was observed and shear bands did not appear to cross as many adjacent grains. For low strains, $\varepsilon=0.6$ and 1.1, the strength of shear banding was observed to be of moderate proportions compared with higher strains. Shear bands were less well formed in general, as was observed from a lower degree of orientation contrast and less grain boundary distortion [see Fig. 2(b)]. For very low strain levels, $\varepsilon=0.25$ , shear banding was found to be very weak. In this case, the microstructure was typically characterised by very fine banding (not obvious shear banding) with a few isolated instances of actual shear banding.
4.1.2. The effect of deformation temperature.
The effect of deformation temperature on shear band formation was evaluated by performing plane strain compression tests at a fixed strain rate of $\mathrm{~1~s~}^{-1}$ and at strain levels varying from $\varepsilon=0.25$ to 3.1. The results of these tests are summarised in Fig. 3. From Fig. 3 it can be seen that deformation temperature has very little effect on shear band formation for temperatures up to $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for all strains. At $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ some weakening was observed, while for temperatures of $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and higher a significant weakening was observed for all strains. This trend is further reflected in Fig. 4, which reveals the extent of shear band formation for a strain of $\varepsilon=2.7$ and deformation temperatures equal to room temperature, $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The micrographs corresponding to deformation temperatures of room temperature and $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [Fig. 4(a) and (b), respectively] reveal little difference in the strength of the shear bands. The micrograph corresponding to a deformation temperature of $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [Fig. 4(c)] shows a small weakening in comparison, while that corresponding to a deformation temperature of $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [Fig. 4(d)] shows a significant weakening to the extent that shear bands are not visible from optical microscopy analysis. There thus seems to be a critical value of deformation temperature, or range of deformation temperatures, above which the formation of shear bands is significantly impeded for all values of strain. For a strain rate of $1~\mathrm{{s}^{-1}}$ this critical temperature lies between $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
The dislocation structures corresponding to strong shear band formation, some weakening of shear band formation and very weak shear band formation can be inferred from the TEM micrographs in Fig. 5(a), (b) and (c), respectively. Longitudinal views that contain the rolling and normal directions are shown. The dislocation structures corresponding to strong shear band formation after deformation at room temperature (strain rate of $\mathrm{~1~s^{-1}~}$ and strain of $\varepsilon=1.1\$ ) are shown in Fig. 5(a). A distinct microband structure composed of parallel dislocation walls (lamellae) can be observed with the bands aligned at approximately $35^{\circ}$ to the rolling direction. A macroband, presumably a shear band, aligned at the opposite $35^{\circ}$ angle to the rolling direction can also be observed. The dislocation structures corresponding to some weakening of shear band formation after deformation at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (strain rate of $1~\mathrm{{s}}^{-1}$ and strain of $\varepsilon=1.1$ ) are shown in Fig. 5(b). A microband structure may still be observed, although less well developed compared with that in Fig. 5(a). The dislocation structures corresponding to very weak shear band formation after deformation at $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (strain rate of $\mathrm{~1~s~}^{-1}$ and strain of $\varepsilon=1.1$ ) are shown in Fig. 5(c). In contrast to Fig. 5(a) and (b), Fig. 5(c) reveals an equiaxed subgrain structure with no discernible microband formation.
