Figure 9(c) shows the effect of deformation temperature on the deformation textures. With increasing deformation temperature the textures sharpen noticeably, as has already been reported several times in hot-deformed Al alloys (e.g., [36–38]). This general texture sharpening is attributed to a healing out of the dislocations within the subgrains, as well as a consumption of subgrains with scattered orientations during the dynamic recovery that is generally enhanced at higher deformation temperatures. Those mechanisms lead to a reduction in the average orientation scatter of the deformation texture components and thus to an overall increase in texture sharpness [39]. Furthermore, a slight preference of the Bs orientation at the expense of the $\mathtt{C u}$ orientation is apparent from Fig. 9(c).
## 5. DISCUSSION
## 5.1. Shear band formation
Shear banding was observed to increase in strength as strain increases (Figs 2 and 3). This is in agreement with what has been reported by a number of researchers [5, 7, 11, 13, 21] and reflects the continued accommodation of strain by shear bands. At low strain levels, shear bands were observed to be generally restricted to single grains while at high amounts of strain shear bands were commonly observed to cross grain boundaries. This would tend to confirm the transition of the nature of shear bands from crystallographic to noncrystallographic as strain increases, as suggested by Korbel and Martin [10].
The outstanding result of this study is the observed abrupt change in shear band formation behaviour as a function of the combined influence of deformation temperature and strain rate represented by the single parameter, Z (Figs 3, 4 and 6). This abrupt change in shear band formation behaviour coincides very closely with the observed sharp drop in as-deformed hardness and work hardening (Fig. 7), and therefore a sharp increase in dynamic recovery, as a function of decreasing Z. The transition from strong to very weak shear band formation has been narrowed down to the $Z$ range between $9.2{\times}10^{13}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $1.3{\times}10^{14}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , while the inferred sharp increase in dynamic recovery occurs at about $Z=1.0{\times}10^{14}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . Furthermore, the as-deformed detailed microstructures corresponding to strong and very weak shear banding cases are very different. The microstructure corresponding to strong shear banding [Fig. 5(a)] possesses a high dislocation density and is characterised by parallel dislocation walls or microband lamellae. The microstructure corresponding to very weak shear banding [Fig. 5(c)] exhibits a lower relative dislocation density and is characterised by equiaxed subgrains similar to what is normally observed in a dynamically recovered structure corresponding to pure aluminium. These microstructural observations are well supported by similar observations made by Nakayama and Morii [7].
It would appear then that the sharp increase in dynamic recovery as a function of decreasing $Z$ causes a change in the development of deformation microstructure from microband formation to equiaxed subgrain formation. This in turn causes a severe weakening of shear band formation. The supposition that microbands are a necessary precursor to shear band formation as suggested by other workers [7, 10,
12, 13] is thus strongly supported. In addition, the very close coincidence of the step-like increase in dynamic recovery and the virtual disappearance of microbanding and shear banding offers some insight into microband formation.
Alternatively, the abrupt change in hardness might, in principle, be caused by dynamic recrystallisation taking place during the plane strain compression tests. Dynamic recrystallisation has been reported under very special circumstances in Al alloys containing high amounts of $\mathbf{Mg}$ (see, e.g., [40]). However, in most Al alloys dynamic recrystallisation is commonly assumed to be suppressed by dynamic recovery. Accordingly, neither the as-deformed microstructure nor the flow stress data showed any sign of dynamic recrystallisation. Furthermore, the deformation textures showed no evidence of recrystallisation; on the contrary, the texture sharpness increased with decreasing $Z$ [Fig. 9(b)]. Finally, the samples that were subjected to static recrystallisation after the PSC tests revealed the characteristic recrystallisation textures and microstructures of Al alloys, which is also inconsistent with dynamic recrystallisation. These experiments will be addressed in detail in a subsequent paper [23].
Dynamic recovery involves dislocation dipole annihilation and dislocation rearrangements into more stable (lower energy) configurations such as lowangle boundaries in subgrain formation. These processes are facilitated by dislocation movement in the form of glide, climb and cross-slip. Higher deformation temperatures and lower strain rates provide more thermal energy and time for this dislocation movement. However, if dislocation movement is impeded, then the dynamic recovery processes are prevented or significantly reduced. The addition of magnesium to aluminium results in the pinning of otherwise mobile dislocations. This pinning of dislocations is thought to be responsible for the Portevin– Le Chatelier dynamic strain ageing behaviour typical of $\mathbf{Al-Mg} $ alloys [41]. It is also thought to be responsible for the prevention of subgrain formation during the deformation of $\mathbf{Al-Mg} $ alloys at low temperatures and/or high strain rates. It could be that microbands form instead by the co-operative movement of these pinned dislocations [5]. The unpinning of dislocations from $\mathbf{Mg}$ atom atmospheres obviously requires a critical amount of energy and time, or temperature and strain rate in terms of deformation variables. Once the dislocations are unpinned then normal subgrain formation can take place during dynamic recovery.
It should be expected that at least some degree of increased dynamic recovery will take place up to the point of dislocation unpinning, as deformation temperatures and strain rates increase and decrease respectively. This is in fact what has been observed. Microband and shear band formation were both observed to weaken to some extent as a function of decreasing $Z$ up to the critical value of $Z=1.0{\times}10^{14}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . For instance, the dislocation microstructure of the specimen deformed at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(Z=1.7{\times}10^{14}~\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ that is shown in Fig. 5(b) reveals a weakened (although still very distinct) microband structure compared with that of the specimen deformed at room temperature $(Z=2.2{\times}10^{27}~\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ shown in Fig. 5(a).
## 5.2. Deformation textures
The formation of copper-type shear bands is believed to coincide with certain features of deformation texture. Detailed $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray macrotexture measurements have suggested a stabilisation of the Goss orientation, and in particular an increase of the Bs orientation, for both Al–1.8Cu and $\mathbf{Al-}3\mathbf{Mg}$ alloys as strain—and, consequently, the extent of copper-type shear banding—increased [13, 15, 16]. The ODFs of the present cold-deformed specimens indeed displayed quite strong intensities of the Bs component [Fig. 9(a)]—notably stronger than what would be expected for homogeneous deformation starting from the given initial texture—which may well be attributed to the shear banding. From single-crystal experiments it is known that among the main rolling texture components the $\mathtt{C u}$ orientation is most susceptible to shear band formation [7]. Local texture analysis by means of the electron backscatter diffraction (EBSD) technique in cold-rolled $A l{-}3\%$ Mg polycrystals confirmed that shear banding is largely restricted to grains with orientations along the $\upbeta$ -fibre of the rolling texture between the $\mathtt{C u}$ and S orientation, whereas the other $\upbeta$ -fibre orientations between S and Bs orientation and the $\upalpha$ -fibre orientations between Bs and Goss deform homogeneously [8].
Single-crystal experiments performed in $\mathrm{Cu}$ -oriented crystals of copper and shear banding Al alloys have shown that shear band formation is associated with a simple $\pm$ rotation of the original copper orientation about the transverse direction, TD, leading to the Goss orientation {011}100 as well as the $45^{\circ}.$ - rotated cube orientation, {001}110 [15]. A more comprehensive experimental and theoretical study suggested that the shear bands are in fact composed of bundles of microscopic shear bands that experience a positive TD rotation towards the {001}110 orientation, and intermediate regions between the microscopic shear bands that experience a reactive negative TD rotation towards the Goss orientation [12]. Upon further homogeneous deformation the Goss-oriented regions rotate towards the Bs orientation, whereas orientations in the vicinity of the ND-rotated cube orientation tend to rotate towards the $\upalpha$ -fibre and, finally, towards Bs as well [35]. This would account for the increase in this particular orientation as compared with the typical cold-rolling textures of nonshear banding Al alloys (e.g., [13, 15, 16]).
With respect to the texture changes introduced by the shear bands, in the present study another effect may be of some importance. The special geometry of the deformation of a given crystal containing unidirectional shear bands induces a rigid-body rotation by an angle $\phi$ of the affected volume about the TD (Fig. 10) [12, 25]. For the $\mathtt{C u}$ orientation, which is most affected by shear banding, this manifests itself in a shift in the Euler angle $\boldsymbol{\phi}$ towards slightly larger angles $\Phi+\phi$ [14]. Such an orientation change in the position of the ${\mathfrak{\beta}}$ -fibre orientations has indeed been observed in the present study [Fig. 9(b), (d)]. For comparison, the curve obtained from non-shear banding commercial-purity aluminium (AA1145) has been included [42]. Although it must be noted that the exact position of the $\upbeta$ -fibre may be affected by the initial texture of the given material, it is obvious that the curves $\Phi(\varphi_{2})$ of the cold-deformed specimens— where shear band formation is most pronounced—is significantly shifted by a few degrees with respect to that of AA1145. This not only affects the $\mathtt{C u}$ orientation, but all the $\upbeta$ -fibre orientations between the $\mathtt{C u}$ and the S orientation, which confirms that all of these orientations may be affected by shear banding (see [8]). With increasing deformation temperature, where shear band formation became less pronounced, the curves $\Phi(\varphi_{2})$ notably shift back towards the behaviour of pure Al [Fig. 9(d)].
However, whereas with increasing deformation temperature the tendency to form shear bands clearly decreased (Figs 3 and 4), the intensity of the ${\mathfrak{\beta}}$ -fibre shifted further towards the Bs orientation [Fig. 9(c)]. In that context it has to be noted that an increase in deformation temperature has also been reported to favour the Bs orientation, such that the two effects may overlap. The increase in Bs orientation with deformation temperature has been attributed to the increase of the strain-rate sensitivity index [43] and, at very high deformation temperatures, to the additional activation of other, non-octahedral slip systems [44].
## 6. CONCLUSIONS
Copper-type shear bands were observed to occur in Al–1Mg. Their formation has been characterised as a function of the deformation variables of strain, deformation temperature and strain rate by a systematic matrix of PSC testing. Shear band formation was stronger and more frequent with increasing strain. Shear band formation depended critically on deformation temperature and strain rate, i.e., on the Zener– Hollomon parameter $Z.$ , in that a significant weakening of formation was observed over a very narrow range of $Z$ values at $Z$ approximately equal to $1.0{\times}10^{14}~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . This significant weakening was attributed to a sharp increase in dynamic recovery over the same narrow range of $Z$ values, which led to the formation of a subgrain microstructure instead of a microband microstructure that is considered a prerequisite for shear band formation. The formation of shear bands was also found to affect the deformation textures. The relative intensities along the $\upbeta$ -fibre, as well as the precise position of the $\upbeta$ -fibre, were observed to be influenced by the shear band formation.
